WEBVTT
NOTE what-is-hydrothermal-vents - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.753
A hydrothermal vent is an opening in the
seafloor where seawater that has soaked

00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:09.328
down into hot rock flows back out, heated
and loaded with dissolved minerals.

00:00:09.328 --> 00:00:14.720
NOAA puts the hottest of that fluid at up
to 400 degrees Celsius, and on the deep

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.384
seafloor it stays liquid.

00:00:16.384 --> 00:00:18.978
That number tells you how hot a vent can
run.

00:00:18.978 --> 00:00:23.877
It does not tell you why the water refuses
to boil, where the heat comes from, or how

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:27.451
whole communities of animals live beside
it in total darkness.

00:00:27.451 --> 00:00:32.589
Those answers were assembled piece by
piece, from instruments, samples, and one

00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:34.475
discovery nobody planned for.

00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:36.347
What is a hydrothermal vent?

00:00:36.347 --> 00:00:38.821
Vents were expected before they were
found.

00:00:38.821 --> 00:00:43.598
By the time of the first expedition, the
theory of seafloor spreading held that new

00:00:43.598 --> 00:00:48.087
ocean crust forms along mid-ocean ridges,
where tectonic plates pull apart and

00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:50.389
molten rock rises close to the seafloor.

00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:55.082
If that was right, cold ocean water should
be seeping into the young, cracked rock,

00:00:55.082 --> 00:00:57.061
warming up, and escaping somewhere.

00:00:57.061 --> 00:01:02.421
Instruments towed above the Galápagos Rift
had recorded faint pulses of warmer water.

00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:07.828
So in 1977, scientists dove in the
submersible Alvin, operated by the Woods

00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:11.504
Hole Oceanographic Institution, to find
the source.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.320
They found the warm springs.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.767
Around them they also found something the
expedition had not been designed to study:

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:23.307
dense clusters of clams, mussels and tube
worms, thriving in the dark.

00:01:23.307 --> 00:01:28.350
The team was made up mostly of geologists
and geochemists, and the biology arrived

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.211
as a surprise.

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:34.452
Two years later, in 1979, Alvin dives on
the East Pacific Rise found something

00:01:34.452 --> 00:01:34.923
hotter.

00:01:34.923 --> 00:01:38.824
Tall mineral chimneys were pouring out
what looked like thick black smoke.

00:01:38.824 --> 00:01:44.576
The temperature probe the team carried was
not built for it; by WHOI's account, part

00:01:44.576 --> 00:01:45.467
of it melted.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:49.837
Those first black smokers turned a
predicted phenomenon into one of the most

00:01:49.837 --> 00:01:52.309
closely studied features of the deep
ocean.

00:01:52.309 --> 00:01:55.615
Here is the sequence as geochemists
reconstruct it.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:02:00.347
First, cold seawater seeps down through
cracks in the crust near a ridge.

00:02:00.347 --> 00:02:04.768
Deeper in, that water is heated by magma
sitting below the seafloor.

00:02:04.768 --> 00:02:07.819
Hot water under pressure is chemically
aggressive.

00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:12.579
As it moves through rock it gives up some
of its own dissolved ingredients and

00:02:12.579 --> 00:02:17.278
dissolves metals and sulfide in their
place: iron, copper, zinc, and hydrogen

00:02:17.278 --> 00:02:18.437
sulfide among them.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:22.969
Hot fluid is buoyant, so it rises and
exits through the seafloor, often along

00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:24.971
the same fractures that let it in.

00:02:24.971 --> 00:02:29.675
When that fluid meets bottom water just
above freezing, much of what it carries

00:02:29.675 --> 00:02:31.461
precipitates almost instantly.

00:02:31.461 --> 00:02:33.666
That is what the black smoke is.

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:38.972
NOAA describes it as a cloud of mineral
particles, mostly metal sulfides, not

00:02:38.972 --> 00:02:40.832
smoke in any burning sense.

00:02:40.832 --> 00:02:45.480
Some of those particles settle around the
opening, and layer by layer they build a

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.331
hollow chimney.

00:02:46.331 --> 00:02:48.927
Chimneys grow, crack, topple and regrow.

00:02:48.927 --> 00:02:53.730
Where hot fluid has already mixed with
seawater underground, the vent runs

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.339
cooler, carries lighter-colored minerals,
and is called a white smoker.

00:02:58.339 --> 00:03:00.386
Other vents build no chimney at all.

00:03:00.386 --> 00:03:04.765
They appear only as shimmering water
rising from cracks, called diffuse flow.

00:03:04.765 --> 00:03:09.171
No one has watched seawater travel down
through the crust, so that leg of the loop

00:03:09.171 --> 00:03:09.816
is inferred.

00:03:09.816 --> 00:03:11.954
The strongest evidence is chemical.

00:03:11.954 --> 00:03:16.963
Vent fluid collected by submersibles and
robotic vehicles comes up almost stripped

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:20.872
of magnesium, an element seawater normally
carries in abundance.

00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:25.975
The simplest explanation is that the water
reacted with hot rock along the way and

00:03:25.975 --> 00:03:28.589
left its magnesium behind in new minerals.

00:03:28.589 --> 00:03:33.434
Seismic surveys that image magma beneath
ridges, and laboratory experiments that

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:36.947
heat seawater with crushed basalt, point
to the same loop.

00:03:36.947 --> 00:03:41.044
At sea level, pure water boils at 100
degrees Celsius.

00:03:41.044 --> 00:03:43.928
That is not a property of water alone.

00:03:43.928 --> 00:03:48.200
It is a property of water under the weight
of one atmosphere of air.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.872
On the deep seafloor, the whole ocean
above presses down, and the pressure there

00:03:52.872 --> 00:03:55.267
is many times higher than at the surface.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:58.837
The boiling point is set by pressure, and
it climbs with it.

00:03:58.837 --> 00:04:04.899
That is why fluid near 400 degrees can
leave a chimney as a liquid rather than as

00:04:04.899 --> 00:04:06.171
a burst of steam.

00:04:06.171 --> 00:04:09.399
Pressure raises the bar, but it does not
remove it.

00:04:09.399 --> 00:04:14.653
Under some combinations of temperature and
depth, vent fluid does boil underground,

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:19.147
or separates into a lighter, vapor-like
phase and a dense, salty brine.

00:04:19.147 --> 00:04:23.621
Scientists detect this after the fact, in
the saltiness of what comes out.

00:04:23.621 --> 00:04:28.508
Some vents release fluid fresher than
seawater and others release fluid saltier

00:04:28.508 --> 00:04:33.146
than seawater, and that difference is a
fingerprint of separation happening

00:04:33.146 --> 00:04:34.136
somewhere below.

00:04:34.136 --> 00:04:39.242
At the hottest and deepest extremes, the
distinction between liquid and gas stops

00:04:39.242 --> 00:04:40.755
being meaningful at all.

00:04:40.755 --> 00:04:44.432
Almost every food web you know begins with
photosynthesis.

00:04:44.432 --> 00:04:49.123
Sunlight fades quickly with depth, and at
most vents there is none at all.

00:04:49.123 --> 00:04:52.028
Life there runs instead on chemosynthesis.

00:04:52.028 --> 00:04:57.492
Microbes capture energy from chemical
reactions, most famously by oxidizing the

00:04:57.492 --> 00:05:02.956
hydrogen sulfide in vent fluid, and use
that energy to build sugars from carbon

00:05:02.956 --> 00:05:03.509
dioxide.

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:08.176
Some live free in the water or on rock.
Others live inside animals.

00:05:08.176 --> 00:05:12.256
Either way, these microbes are the base of
the vent food web.

00:05:12.256 --> 00:05:17.235
The giant tube worm, Riftia pachyptila,
shows how far that partnership can go.

00:05:17.235 --> 00:05:19.338
Adults have no mouth and no gut.

00:05:19.338 --> 00:05:24.268
Their red plume, colored by hemoglobin,
takes up oxygen, carbon dioxide and

00:05:24.268 --> 00:05:27.160
hydrogen sulfide from the surrounding
water.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.580
The blood carries those to an organ called
the trophosome, packed with symbiotic

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.005
bacteria that run the chemistry and feed
the worm from within.

00:05:35.005 --> 00:05:39.469
The animal itself lives inside a tough
white tube anchored to the rock.

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:45.043
Biologists did not take that arrangement
on trust. Several lines of evidence agree.

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:48.374
Dissection showed there is no mouth and no
gut.

00:05:48.374 --> 00:05:53.901
Microscopy showed bacteria inside the
trophosome, densely packed in its cells.

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:58.513
Enzyme measurements found the chemical
machinery for fixing carbon using energy

00:05:58.513 --> 00:05:59.272
from sulfide.

00:05:59.272 --> 00:06:04.884
And carbon signatures in the worm's tissue
match carbon fixed by those bacteria, not

00:06:04.884 --> 00:06:08.024
carbon from food sinking down from the
surface.

00:06:08.024 --> 00:06:10.881
Any one of these could be questioned on
its own.

00:06:10.881 --> 00:06:14.035
Together, they show the worm is fed by its
symbionts.

00:06:14.035 --> 00:06:17.201
Vent communities also differ from ocean to
ocean.

00:06:17.201 --> 00:06:20.109
Giant tube worms dominate many Pacific
sites.

00:06:20.109 --> 00:06:24.631
On the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, swarming shrimp
crowd the chimneys instead.

00:06:24.631 --> 00:06:29.928
In the Indian Ocean, the scaly-foot snail
grows a shell layered with iron sulfide.

00:06:29.928 --> 00:06:34.241
Biologists explain these differences
partly by how larvae disperse between vent

00:06:34.241 --> 00:06:37.517
fields that can be far apart, and partly
by local chemistry.

00:06:37.517 --> 00:06:42.952
How exactly animals colonize a newly
formed vent remains an active question.

00:06:42.952 --> 00:06:45.703
Black smokers are not the whole story.

00:06:45.703 --> 00:06:51.423
In 2000, researchers exploring near the
Mid-Atlantic Ridge came upon a field of

00:06:51.423 --> 00:06:55.405
pale, towering chimneys that became known
as Lost City.

00:06:55.405 --> 00:07:00.578
NOAA Ocean Exploration has documented the
site with remotely operated vehicles.

00:07:00.578 --> 00:07:05.293
Its chimneys are made of carbonate
minerals, not metal sulfides, and its

00:07:05.293 --> 00:07:10.728
fluid is far cooler than a black smoker's
and strongly alkaline rather than acidic.

00:07:10.728 --> 00:07:12.561
The heat source is different too.

00:07:12.561 --> 00:07:16.781
Lost City sits on mantle rock that has
been pushed up close to the seafloor.

00:07:16.781 --> 00:07:21.646
When seawater reacts with that rock, in a
process called serpentinization, the

00:07:21.646 --> 00:07:25.576
reaction itself releases heat, along with
hydrogen and methane.

00:07:25.576 --> 00:07:30.669
So Lost City is driven by rock chemistry,
not driven by magma.

00:07:30.669 --> 00:07:35.328
That matters because it means vents can
exist away from active volcanic centers,

00:07:35.328 --> 00:07:39.347
and it gives microbes a different menu of
chemical energy to live on.

00:07:39.347 --> 00:07:41.657
Vents also affect the ocean as a whole.

00:07:41.657 --> 00:07:46.158
Seawater is thought to circulate through
hydrothermal systems over very long

00:07:46.158 --> 00:07:50.777
timescales, and in the process vents add
some elements to the ocean and remove

00:07:50.777 --> 00:07:51.192
others.

00:07:51.192 --> 00:07:55.475
Plumes of dissolved iron from vents have
been traced far from their source.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:08:00.629
The minerals left behind form deposits
rich in copper, zinc and other metals,

00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:05.315
which is why vents interest economic
geologists as well as biologists.

00:08:05.315 --> 00:08:11.290
Much of this work is published by public
bodies: NOAA reports on vent temperatures

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:16.609
and expeditions, WHOI on Alvin and the
1977 dives, and the Smithsonian on

00:08:16.609 --> 00:08:17.629
deep-sea life.

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:21.699
Vents also sit at the center of one of
science's open questions.

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:26.399
Some researchers propose that life on
Earth began at hydrothermal vents, where

00:08:26.399 --> 00:08:31.401
chemical energy and mineral surfaces were
both available, and alkaline systems like

00:08:31.401 --> 00:08:33.992
Lost City feature heavily in that
argument.

00:08:33.992 --> 00:08:38.750
Others favor shallow pools on land, where
repeated drying and wetting can

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.184
concentrate molecules.

00:08:40.184 --> 00:08:43.277
Laboratory experiments support pieces of
both ideas.

00:08:43.277 --> 00:08:47.915
Neither has been demonstrated, and you
should treat any confident claim either

00:08:47.915 --> 00:08:49.640
way as ahead of the evidence.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.673
Space is fascinating, but Earth bothers me
more.

00:08:52.673 --> 00:08:56.653
We're already here and still don't
understand huge parts of it.

00:08:56.653 --> 00:09:01.523
As you watch vent footage, remember that
every image is lit by the vehicle itself.

00:09:01.523 --> 00:09:05.962
Without its lamps the scene would be
black, so the colors you see are the

00:09:05.962 --> 00:09:08.760
colors of artificial light on rock and
tissue.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:13.276
The black smoke looks slow and soft, but
it is fluid hot enough to damage

00:09:13.276 --> 00:09:15.069
instruments not built for it.

00:09:15.069 --> 00:09:18.259
Watch, too, for the shimmer above the
cooler cracks.

00:09:18.259 --> 00:09:23.104
That ripple is diffuse flow, warm water
bending the light as it rises, and many

00:09:23.104 --> 00:09:27.459
vent animals live in that milder water
rather than in the hottest jets.

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:30.361
This evidence firmly establishes some
things.

00:09:30.361 --> 00:09:35.456
First, vent fluid stays liquid under
pressure, which follows from basic physics

00:09:35.456 --> 00:09:37.971
and is confirmed by direct measurement.

00:09:37.971 --> 00:09:43.321
Second, tube worms are fed by symbionts,
shown by several independent lines of

00:09:43.321 --> 00:09:43.939
evidence.

00:09:43.939 --> 00:09:48.797
Other things remain open. No one knows
precisely how many vents exist.

00:09:48.797 --> 00:09:54.249
The mid-ocean ridge system is enormous,
only a small fraction has been surveyed

00:09:54.249 --> 00:09:56.664
closely, and estimates vary widely.

00:09:56.664 --> 00:10:01.793
Vents can switch on and off as eruptions
and earthquakes reshape the plumbing, so

00:10:01.793 --> 00:10:03.187
any map is a snapshot.

00:10:03.187 --> 00:10:07.216
And where life began is not settled by
anything in this video.

00:10:07.216 --> 00:10:11.880
A hydrothermal vent is seawater returning
from a journey through hot rock, carrying

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.240
the crust's chemistry back into the ocean.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.832
We know its temperature because
instruments were sent into it, its

00:10:17.832 --> 00:10:22.349
plumbing because of what is missing from
its water, and its life because biologists

00:10:22.349 --> 00:10:24.091
took that life apart and looked.

00:10:24.091 --> 00:10:28.960
Much of the seafloor where such vents
could exist has still never been seen.
